<section id="A3.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.7 </span>Results on SimNPO (Llama-3.1)</h3>

<div id="A3.SS7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A3.T13" title="Table 13 ‣ C.7 Results on SimNPO (Llama-3.1) ‣ Appendix Appendix C Additional Experimental Results ‣ Layer-Targeted Multilingual Knowledge Erasure in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> presents results using SimNPO. At Layer 30 (deep), unlearning fails with high Forget accuracy (81.0%). At Layer 9 (target), the method achieves perfect erasure (0.0%) with high retention (86.0%), demonstrating the most favorable erasure-utility trade-off among the three algorithms evaluated.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.T13" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A3.T13.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 13</span>: </span><span id="A3.T13.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Llama-3.1 (SimNPO) across Layers.<span id="A3.T13.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Comparison of target and deep layers. Deep intervention (L30) fails to erase knowledge, with high Forget accuracy. The target layer (L9) achieves perfect erasure while maintaining strong utility across all languages. Asterisks (*) denote source languages (<math id="A3.T13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{src}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>src</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{src}}</annotation></semantics></math>).</span></span></figcaption>
<div id="A3.T13.6" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:147.6pt;vertical-align:-72.1pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-82.8pt,35.4pt) scale(0.675742610432528,0.675742610432528) ;">
